        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights, Discipline, Manifestation Determination, and Due Process in District 24</h1>

  <p>District 24 is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. Students have rights, and the school district has a responsibility to ensure fair and equitable disciplinary practices. This comprehensive guide explains student rights, disciplinary procedures, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights within District 24. It's designed to empower parents and guardians with the knowledge needed to advocate effectively for their children.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Student Rights in District 24</h2>

  <p>All students in New York State, including those in District 24, are entitled to certain rights, as outlined in the New York State Education Law and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). These rights include the right to a free and appropriate public education (FAPE), the right to due process, and the right to be treated with respect and dignity. For students with disabilities, IDEA provides additional protections, especially when disciplinary actions are considered.</p>

  <h3>Key Student Rights:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE):</b> This is a cornerstone of IDEA.  FAPE means special education and related services are provided at public expense, under public supervision and direction, and without charge; meet the standards of the NYSED; include an appropriate preschool, elementary school, or secondary school education in the state involved; and are provided in conformity with an individualized education program (IEP) as required under Section 614(d) of IDEA. (See 34 CFR §300.17)</li>
        <li><b>Right to Due Process:</b>  Parents have the right to challenge school decisions regarding their child's identification, evaluation, educational placement, or the provision of FAPE. This includes the right to mediation, impartial hearings, and judicial review.</li>
        <li><b>Right to an IEP (if eligible for special education):</b> An Individualized Education Program (IEP) is a legally binding document that outlines a student's specific educational needs, goals, and the services the school will provide to meet those needs.  This is governed by IDEA and NYS Part 200 Regulations.</li>
        <li><b>Right to a 504 Plan (if eligible under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act):</b> A 504 plan provides accommodations and modifications for students with disabilities who do not require special education services but need support to access the general education curriculum.</li>
        <li><b>Right to a Safe and Supportive School Environment:</b>  All students have the right to learn in an environment free from harassment, bullying, and discrimination.</li>
    </ul>

  <h2>Disciplinary Procedures in District 24</h2>

  <p>District 24 follows the NYC Department of Education's citywide Standards of Discipline and Intervention Measures.  These standards outline the types of misconduct that may result in disciplinary action and the range of consequences that may be imposed. It's essential to understand that disciplinary procedures are different for students with and without IEPs or 504 plans, especially regarding suspensions exceeding 10 days.</p>

  <h3>Common Disciplinary Actions:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Verbal Warning:</b> A reminder of expected behavior.</li>
        <li><b>Written Warning:</b> A formal notice of misconduct.</li>
        <li><b>Detention:</b> Supervised time after school.</li>
        <li><b>Suspension (In-School):</b> Removal from regular classes but remaining in a supervised setting within the school.</li>
        <li><b>Suspension (Out-of-School):</b> Removal from school grounds for a specified period.  This is the most serious disciplinary action and triggers specific rights for students with IEPs and 504 Plans.</li>
        <li><b>Transfer to another school within District 24:</b> This is a significant change in placement and requires careful consideration, especially for students with IEPs.</li>
    </ul>

  <h3>Step-by-Step Guide: What to Do If Your Child Faces Disciplinary Action</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Stay Calm and Gather Information:</b>  Speak with your child to get their perspective on the incident.  Request a written explanation of the charges from the school administration. Note the date, time, and names of all individuals involved.</li>
        <li><b>Review the School's Disciplinary Code:</b>  Familiarize yourself with District 24's disciplinary policies and procedures.  These policies should be available on the school's website or from the school administration.</li>
        <li><b>Contact the School:</b>  Schedule a meeting with the principal, guidance counselor, or other relevant school personnel to discuss the situation. Bring all relevant documentation with you.</li>
        <li><b>Document Everything:</b>  Keep a detailed record of all communications with the school, including dates, times, and the content of conversations. Save copies of all documents related to the disciplinary action.</li>
        <li><b>Know Your Rights:</b>  Understand your child's rights, especially if they have an IEP or 504 plan.</li>
        <li><b>Seek Support:</b> Contact legal aid organizations, parent advocacy groups, or a special education attorney for assistance.</li>
    </ol>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDR) - A Detailed Guide</h2>

  <p>As mentioned earlier, a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is required whenever a student with an IEP or 504 plan faces a suspension exceeding 10 school days or a pattern of shorter suspensions that constitute a change of placement.  This review is critical because it determines whether the student's behavior is related to their disability. If the behavior *is* determined to be a manifestation of the disability or a result of the school's failure to implement the IEP/504 plan, the student cannot be disciplined in the same way as a student without a disability.</p>

  <h3>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Notification:</b>  The school must notify you promptly that your child is facing a suspension that triggers an MDR. This notification should include information about your rights and the MDR process.</li>
        <li><b>IEP Team Meeting:</b> An IEP team meeting (or 504 team meeting) must be convened to conduct the MDR. This team includes the parent(s)/guardian(s), school administrators, teachers, and other relevant professionals. The meeting should be scheduled within a reasonable timeframe.</li>
        <li><b>Review of Relevant Information:</b> The IEP team must review all relevant information, including the student's IEP/504 plan, evaluations, observations, and any relevant information provided by the parents.  This review is critical to understanding the student's educational history and the specific supports and services they receive. The team also needs to understand the context and nature of the behavior leading to the disciplinary action.</li>
        <li><b>Determining Manifestation:</b> The team must answer two key questions:
            <ul>
                <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child’s disability?</li>
                <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district’s failure to implement the IEP?</li>
            </ul>
            If the answer to either question is "yes," the behavior is considered a manifestation of the student's disability.</li>
        <li><b>Outcomes of the MDR:</b>
            <ul>
                <li><b>If the behavior *is* a manifestation:</b> The IEP team must conduct a functional behavioral assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement a behavioral intervention plan (BIP) for the student, or review the existing BIP and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior. The student generally returns to their placement unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP.  (See 34 CFR §300.530(f)).  The school can request an expedited due process hearing if they believe maintaining the student's current placement is substantially likely to result in injury to the student or others (34 CFR §300.532(b)).</li>
                <li><b>If the behavior *is not* a manifestation:</b> The student can be disciplined in the same way as a student without a disability. However, the school must continue to provide FAPE to the student during any period of suspension exceeding 10 school days.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>

  <h3>Parent Advocacy Tips for MDRs:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Prepare Thoroughly:</b> Before the MDR meeting, gather all relevant documentation, including your child's IEP/504 plan, evaluations, medical records, and any incident reports.  Prepare a statement outlining your perspective on the incident and why you believe the behavior is related to your child's disability.</li>
        <li><b>Actively Participate:</b>  Attend the MDR meeting and actively participate in the discussion.  Ask questions, share your insights, and advocate for your child's needs. Don't be afraid to disagree with the school if you believe their assessment is inaccurate.</li>
        <li><b>Understand the Legal Standard:</b>  Familiarize yourself with the legal standard for determining manifestation.  Remember that the burden of proof is on the school district to demonstrate that the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the disability.</li>
        <li><b>Request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the school's evaluation or assessment, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (if you meet certain criteria). An IEE can provide an independent perspective on your child's needs.  (See 34 CFR §300.502).</li>
        <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communications with the school, including the MDR meeting.  If you believe the school is not following proper procedures, document your concerns in writing.</li>
        <li><b>Consult with an Advocate or Attorney:</b> If you are facing challenges navigating the MDR process, consider seeking assistance from a special education advocate or attorney.</li>
    </ul>

  <h2>Due Process Rights in District 24</h2>

  <p>Parents have the right to challenge school decisions regarding their child's identification, evaluation, educational placement, or the provision of FAPE. This right is guaranteed by IDEA and is referred to as "due process." If you disagree with the school's decisions, you have several options, including mediation and impartial hearings.</p>

  <h3>Steps to Take When Disputing a School Decision:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><b>Informal Resolution:</b>  Before initiating formal due process procedures, try to resolve the issue informally by meeting with the school administration, teachers, or IEP team. Often, open communication and collaboration can lead to a mutually agreeable solution.</li>
        <li><b>Mediation:</b> Mediation is a voluntary process in which a neutral third party helps parents and the school district reach a mutually agreeable resolution. The New York State Education Department offers mediation services at no cost to parents.</li>
        <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> If mediation is unsuccessful or not appropriate, you have the right to request an impartial hearing. An impartial hearing is a formal legal proceeding in which an impartial hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision. You have the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and be represented by an attorney or advocate.  The decision of the Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO) is binding unless appealed.</li>
        <li><b>Judicial Review:</b>  If you disagree with the decision of the impartial hearing officer, you have the right to appeal the decision to a state or federal court.</li>
    </ol>

  <h3>Common District 24 Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Failure to Conduct Timely MDRs:</b>  Ensure the school schedules the MDR meeting promptly after a triggering event (suspension exceeding 10 days or a pattern of shorter suspensions).  Document any delays and follow up with the school administration.</li>
        <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b>  Monitor the implementation of your child's IEP closely.  If you notice that the school is not providing the services and supports outlined in the IEP, document the discrepancies and communicate your concerns to the school.</li>
        <li><b>Lack of Parent Involvement:</b>  Actively participate in all IEP meetings and other school-related activities.  Maintain open communication with the school staff and advocate for your child's needs.</li>
        <li><b>Insufficient Functional Behavioral Assessments (FBAs):</b>  If your child has behavioral challenges, ensure that the school conducts a comprehensive FBA to identify the triggers and functions of the behavior. The FBA should be used to develop a BIP that addresses the underlying causes of the behavior.</li>
        <li><b>Reliance on Punitive Discipline:</b> Advocate for positive behavioral interventions and supports (PBIS) that focus on teaching appropriate behavior and preventing problems before they occur.  Challenge the use of suspension and other punitive measures as the primary means of addressing behavior.</li>
    </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) about Discipline and Special Education in District 24</h2>

  <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> An IEP is for students who require specialized instruction due to their disability. A 504 plan provides accommodations for students with disabilities who can access the general education curriculum with some support.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What happens if my child's behavior is a manifestation of their disability?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> The IEP team must conduct an FBA and develop a BIP to address the behavior. Your child generally returns to their current placement, unless the team agrees to a change of placement.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the school's decision about my child's discipline?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> You have the right to challenge the school's decision through mediation or an impartial hearing.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York State?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> You can find information on the NYSED website (http://www.nysed.gov/) and through organizations like the INCLUDEnyc.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be expelled from school if they have an IEP?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Expulsion is rare for students with IEPs, but it is possible in certain circumstances. However, an MDR must be conducted before any expulsion proceedings can begin.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: My child has received multiple short-term suspensions. Will this trigger an MDR?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Yes, a series of short-term suspensions that constitute a pattern and total more than 10 days in a school year can trigger an MDR if the suspensions represent a change of placement.</dd>
  </dl>

